\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg[index] heap content\",\"size\":\"unbounded (printf %s)\",\"note\":\"printf('%s', msg[index])\"}],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"scanf\",\"_abort\"],\"behavior\":\"Reads index as %llu. Checks index < cookie_num using SIGNED comparison (jl). No lower bound check. Prints msg[index] as string via printf('%s', ptr). Same signed comparison issue as edit_cookie.\"},{\"name\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x14e1\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"_abort\",\"fread\",\"printf\",\"free\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"random_index\",\"source\":\"fread from /dev/urandom, 1 byte, modulo cookie_num\"}],\"checks\":[\"cookie_num != 0 else _abort('No cookie!')\"],\"memory_operations\":[{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"urandom FILE*\",\"size\":1,\"note\":\"fread into rbp-0xc\"},{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg[random_index] @0x4060+random_index*8\",\"size\":8,\"note\":\"printed then freed\"},{\"operation\":\"FREE\",\"target\":\"msg[random_index] heap pointer\",\"note\":\"free(msg[random_index])\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg[random_index] @0x4060+random_index*8\",\"size\":8,\"value\":\"msg[cookie_num-1] (move last to freed slot)\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg_size[random_index] @0x4160+random_index*4\",\"size\":4,\"value\":\"msg_size[cookie_num-1]\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"cookie_num @0x41e8\",\"size\":8,\"value\":\"cookie_num-1\"}],\"calls\":[\"_abort\",\"fread\",\"printf\",\"free\"],\"behavior\":\"Reads 1 byte from /dev/urandom, computes random_index = byte % cookie_num. Prints msg[random_index]. Frees msg[random_index]. Moves msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[random_index] (swap-remove). Moves msg_size[cookie_num-1] into msg_size[random_index]. Decrements cookie_num. NOTE: when random_index == cookie_num-1, the freed pointer is overwritten with itself (same value written back). The old slot msg[cookie_num-1] is NOT zeroed after the move.\"},{\"name\":\"menu\",\"address\":\"0x17fd\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"puts\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[],\"checks\":[],\"memory_operations\":[],\"calls\":[\"puts x7\"],\"behavior\":\"Prints the 7-line fortune cookie menu.\"},{\"name\":\"_abort\",\"address\":\"0x11f5\",\"callers\":[\"init\",\"create_cookie\",\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\",\"eat_cookie\"],\"callees\":[\"printf\",\"exit\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"msg\",\"source\":\"rdi (string literal)\"}],\"checks\":[],\"memory_operations\":[],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"exit(1)\"],\"behavior\":\"Prints error message and calls exit(1).\"}],\"attack_surfaces\":[{\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"input\":\"index (scanf %llu, unsigned 64-bit)\",\"operation\":\"signed comparison jl for index < cookie_num; no lower bound; index used to address msg[index] and msg_size[index]\",\"risk\":\"HIGH\",\"reason\":\"Attacker-controlled index read as unsigned %llu but compared with signed jl. A large unsigned value (high bit set, e.g. 0xffffffffffffffff = -1 signed) passes the signed < cookie_num check. This index is then used to compute msg+index*8 (pointer dereference for read destination) and msg_size+index*4 (read length). This reaches pointer dereference and heap write with attacker-controlled index.\"},{\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"input\":\"index (scanf %llu, unsigned 64-bit)\",\"operation\":\"signed comparison jl for index < cookie_num; no lower bound; index used to address msg[index] for printf %s\",\"risk\":\"HIGH\",\"reason\":\"Same signed comparison issue as edit_cookie. Attacker-controlled index reaches msg+index*8 pointer dereference, and the pointed-to value is used as a string pointer for printf. This is a potential arbitrary read via controlled pointer dereference.\"},{\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"input\":\"random_index (from /dev/urandom)\",\"operation\":\"free(msg[random_index]) then msg[random_index]=msg[cookie_num-1] without zeroing old slot\",\"risk\":\"MEDIUM\",\"reason\":\"After free, the freed pointer's slot is overwritten with msg[cookie_num-1]. If random_index == cookie_num-1, the freed pointer is written back to the same slot (dangling pointer remains). The old msg[cookie_num-1] slot is not zeroed. Potential for use-after-free if the moved pointer can be accessed via another path, but index is random (not attacker-controlled). S2 should analyze whether the swap-remove logic creates a dangling pointer scenario.\"},{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"input\":\"msg_len (scanf %lld)\",\"operation\":\"calloc(msg_len,1) then read(0, buf, msg_len)\",\"risk\":\"LOW\",\"reason\":\"msg_len is checked <= 0x100. calloc(msg_len,1) with msg_len=0 would allocate a minimum chunk; read(0, buf, 0) reads 0 bytes. No obvious overflow. However, msg_len is signed %lld and checked with jle 0x100, so negative values would pass the check but calloc with negative (large unsigned) would likely fail. S2 should verify edge cases.\"}],\"facts\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Binary is ELF64 amd64 PIE, dynamically linked, not stripped, compiled with GCC 10.2.0\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F002\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Security: Full RELRO, NX enabled, Canary, PIE enabled, no FORTIFY, no CET (IBT/SHSTK)\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F003\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"libc is glibc 2.39 (Ubuntu GLIBC 2.39-0ubuntu8.8) at /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6\",\"source\":\"RUNTIME\"},{\"id\":\"F004\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"BSS layout: msg[32] (char* array) @0x4060 size 256B, msg_size[32] (int array) @0x4160 size 128B, urandom (FILE*) @0x41e0, cookie_num (long) @0x41e8\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F005\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg and msg_size are parallel arrays: msg[i] holds heap pointer, msg_size[i] holds allocation size. Both indexed identically by cookie functions.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F006\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"init pre-creates 5 cookies via strdup with sizes [0x50,0x30,0x18,0x38,0x40] and sets cookie_num=5\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F007\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"create_cookie checks cookie_num<=31 and msg_len<=256, allocates calloc(msg_len,1), reads msg_len bytes, stores in msg[cookie_num], increments cookie_num\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F008\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie reads index as %llu (unsigned), checks index<cookie_num using signed jl (cmp rdx,rax; jl). No lower bound check. Uses index to compute msg[index] (pointer deref) and msg_size[index]-1 (read length).\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F009\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"read_cookie reads index as %llu (unsigned), checks index<cookie_num using signed jl. No lower bound check. Dereferences msg[index] and prints as string via printf %s.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F010\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie: random_index = fread_byte % cookie_num. Frees msg[random_index]. Moves msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[random_index] and msg_size[cookie_num-1] into msg_size[random_index]. Old msg[cookie_num-1] slot NOT zeroed. Decrements cookie_num.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F011\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"GOT is at 0x3f58, Full RELRO makes it read-only at runtime\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F012\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"alarm(60) set in init, program will receive SIGALRM after 60 seconds\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F013\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"main reads choice as %lld (signed 64-bit) via scanf\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F014\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie read size is msg_size[index]-1 (sub eax,0x1 then movsxd), read destination is msg[index] heap buffer\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F015\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"The signed jl comparison on an unsigned %llu input in edit_cookie/read_cookie allows indices with high bit set (>= 0x8000000000000000) to pass the < cookie_num check, enabling out-of-bounds array access\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F016\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"strdup is imported (PLT 0x10f0) and used in init to allocate the 5 initial cookie strings on the heap\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F017\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"create_cookie uses calloc(msg_len, 1) for allocation and read(0, buf, msg_len) for input, with trailing newline stripping\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F018\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie index is random (from /dev/urandom), not attacker-controlled\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"id\":\"U001\",\"description\":\"Whether seccomp or other sandbox is active\",\"reason\":\"No seccomp filter detected in ELF; cannot confirm runtime sandbox without explicit check\"},{\"id\":\"U002\",\"description\":\"Exact heap layout and chunk addresses at runtime\",\"reason\":\"ASLR + heap randomization; requires dynamic analysis to determine\"},{\"id\":\"U003\",\"description\":\"Whether the signed comparison in edit_cookie/read_cookie actually allows practical OOB access\",\"reason\":\"The index is read as %llu (unsigned) but compared with signed jl. S2 needs to confirm the exact behavior of large unsigned values passing the signed check and what memory they would reach\"},{\"id\":\"U004\",\"description\":\"Whether eat_cookie's swap-remove creates a usable dangling pointer\",\"reason\":\"When random_index == cookie_num-1, the freed pointer is written back to the same slot. S2 needs to determine if this pointer can be accessed again via edit/read_cookie before being overwritten\"},{\"id\":\"U005\",\"description\":\"Whether create_cookie with msg_len=0 or edge values causes issues\",\"reason\":\"calloc(0,1) returns a valid minimum-size chunk; read(0,buf,0) returns 0. S2 should verify if this creates any exploitable state\"}],\"raw\":{\"elf\":\"ELF64 LSB pie executable x86-64, DYN, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, Entry 0x1110, 11 program headers, 29 sections, not stripped, BuildID 84dab319bf6fe22134cf0338cbd8071fd8bc06c2\",\"sections\":\".text@0x1110(0x891,RX) .rodata@0x2000(0x266,R) .data@0x4000(0x10,RW) .bss@0x4020(0x1d0,RW) .got@0x3f58(0xa8,FullRELRO) .plt@0x1020(0xe0,RX) .init_array@0x3d58 .fini_array@0x3d60 .eh_frame@0x22e0 .eh_frame_hdr@0x2268\",\"symbols\":\"main@0x187f init@0x1232 create_cookie@0x1399 eat_cookie@0x14e1 edit_cookie@0x1628 read_cookie@0x1751 menu@0x17fd _abort@0x11f5 | msg@0x4060(256B) msg_size@0x4160(128B) cookie_num@0x41e8(8B) urandom@0x41e0(8B) stdout@0x4020 stdin@0x4030 stderr@0x4040\",\"disassembly\":\"main: init->menu loop, scanf %lld choice 1-5 dispatch. create_cookie: scanf %lld msg_len(<=256), calloc(msg_len,1), read(0,buf,msg_len), store msg[cookie_num]++ . edit_cookie: scanf %llu index, signed jl check(<cookie_num, no lower bound), read(0,msg[idx],msg_size[idx]-1). read_cookie: scanf %llu index, signed jl check, printf %s msg[idx]. eat_cookie: fread urandom byte%cookie_num, printf msg[idx], free(msg[idx]), swap-remove last->idx, cookie_num--. init: setvbuf x3, alarm(60), fopen /dev/urandom, strdup x5 cookies, cookie_num=5.\"}},\"vulnerability_report\":{\"schema\":\"VulnerabilityReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"total_bugs\":3,\"high_severity\":2,\"medium_severity\":1,\"low_severity\":0},\"vulnerabilities\":[{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"title\":\"OOB_SIGNEDNESS_MISMATCH_IN_INDEX_VALIDATION\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Signedness Error\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie and read_cookie read the cookie index via scanf with format %llu (unsigned 64-bit), but validate it against cookie_num using a signed comparison (cmp rdx,rax; jl). The jl instruction performs a signed less-than comparison. An unsigned input with the high bit set (e.g. 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, which is -1 in signed interpretation) will pass the 'index < cookie_num' check because -1 < 5 (or any positive cookie_num). There is no lower-bound check on the index. The attacker-controlled index is then used to compute addresses into the msg[] pointer array (msg + index*8) and msg_size[] int array (msg_size + index*4), enabling out-of-bounds array access.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg[32] @0x4060 (char* array, 256 bytes)\",\"msg_size[32] @0x4160 (int array, 128 bytes)\",\"cookie_num @0x41e8 (long)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"cookie index via scanf %llu (unsigned 64-bit)\",\"entry_function\":\"edit_cookie / read_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"scanf('%llu', &index) stores unsigned 64-bit value into rbp-0x10\",\"index loaded into rdx at 0x1676 (edit) / 0x179f (read)\",\"cookie_num loaded into rax at 0x167a (edit) / 0x17a3 (read)\",\"cmp rdx,rax at 0x1681 (edit) / 0x17aa (read): compares index and cookie_num as 64-bit values\",\"jl at 0x1684 (edit) / 0x17ad (read): signed jump-if-less — treats both operands as signed\",\"If index has high bit set (>= 0x8000000000000000), it is negative in signed interpretation and passes the < cookie_num check\",\"In edit_cookie: index used at 0x16a7 (lea rdx,[rax*4]) to compute msg_size+index*4 for read length, and at 0x16c3 (lea rcx,[rax*8]) to compute msg+index*8 for read destination pointer\",\"In read_cookie: index used at 0x17bf (lea rdx,[rax*8]) to compute msg+index*8 for pointer dereference, then printf('%s', msg[index])\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"Index should be validated as an unsigned value in range [0, cookie_num-1], using unsigned comparison (jb/jae) and checking both lower bound (>= 0) and upper bound (< cookie_num).\",\"actual\":\"Index is read as unsigned %llu but compared with signed jl instruction. Only upper bound is checked (via signed comparison). No lower bound check exists.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing lower-bound check AND incorrect comparison type: signed jl used instead of unsigned jb. The signedness mismatch allows negative-valued (high-bit-set) unsigned inputs to bypass the upper bound check.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"Out-of-bounds array access via attacker-controlled index\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684 (edit_cookie comparison); 0x16a7-0x16de (edit_cookie OOB access); 0x17aa-0x17ad (read_cookie comparison); 0x17bf-0x17e1 (read_cookie OOB access)\",\"expression\":\"edit_cookie: msg[index] (at msg+index*8) dereferenced as write destination; msg_size[index] (at msg_size+index*4) used as read length. read_cookie: msg[index] (at msg+index*8) dereferenced as string pointer for printf.\",\"target\":\"msg[] array @0x4060 (OOB read to obtain pointer), msg_size[] array @0x4160 (OOB read to obtain size), heap buffer pointed by OOB-obtained pointer (OOB write in edit_cookie)\",\"size\":\"edit_cookie: up to msg_size[index]-1 bytes written to OOB-obtained pointer; read_cookie: unbounded string read via printf %s\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In both edit_cookie and read_cookie, the attacker-controlled index causes OOB read from the msg[] array (at msg+index*8). The value read from OOB memory is treated as a pointer. In read_cookie, this pointer is passed to printf('%s', ptr), causing dereference and string output — potential information disclosure of memory contents at the OOB-derived pointer address. In edit_cookie, OOB reads from both msg[] and msg_size[] arrays obtain a pointer and a size value from out-of-bounds memory.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In edit_cookie, the OOB-obtained pointer (from msg[index] where index is negative/OOB) is used as the destination for read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1). If the OOB memory contains a valid-looking pointer, attacker-supplied data from stdin is written to that address. The write size is msg_size[index]-1, also obtained from OOB memory. This constitutes a potential out-of-bounds/foreign memory write.\"},{\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In read_cookie, the OOB-obtained pointer from msg[index] is passed to printf('%s', ptr). If the OOB memory contains a value that maps to a readable address, the contents at that address are printed as a string, potentially leaking sensitive memory contents such as heap addresses, stack data, or other pointers.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the OOB-obtained pointer value does not map to a valid memory region, dereferencing it (in read_cookie via printf, or in edit_cookie via read) will cause SIGSEGV.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1671\",\"description\":\"scanf called with format string '%llu' (at 0x21c4 in .rodata), storing unsigned 64-bit index into rbp-0x10\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684\",\"description\":\"cmp rdx,rax followed by jl 0x1692 — signed less-than comparison. rdx holds the unsigned index, rax holds cookie_num. jl treats operands as signed, so any index with bit 63 set (negative in signed) passes the < cookie_num check.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a7-0x16b6\",\"description\":\"lea rdx,[rax*4+0x0] then lea rax,[rip+0x2aaa] (msg_size @0x4160) then mov eax,DWORD PTR [rdx+rax*1] — computes msg_size+index*4 and reads 4 bytes. With negative index, this accesses memory before msg_size array.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16c3-0x16d2\",\"description\":\"lea rcx,[rax*8+0x0] then lea rax,[rip+0x298e] (msg @0x4060) then mov rax,QWORD PTR [rcx+rax*1] — computes msg+index*8 and reads 8 bytes as pointer. With negative index, this accesses memory before msg array.\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16bc-0x16de\",\"description\":\"movsxd rdx,eax (sign-extends msg_size[index]-1 to 64-bit) then read(0, msg[index], rdx) — writes attacker data to OOB-obtained pointer with OOB-obtained size\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x179a\",\"description\":\"scanf called with format string '%llu' (at 0x21c4), storing unsigned 64-bit index into rbp-0x10\"},{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17aa-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"cmp rdx,rax followed by jl 0x17bb — same signed comparison as edit_cookie. Unsigned index with high bit set passes the check.\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bf-0x17e1\",\"description\":\"lea rdx,[rax*8+0x0] then lea rax,[rip+0x2892] (msg @0x4060) then mov rax,[rdx+rax*1] — computes msg+index*8, reads 8-byte pointer, then printf('%s', ptr) dereferences it. OOB access with negative index reaches memory before msg array.\"},{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie/read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1684/0x17ad\",\"description\":\"The BSS layout shows msg @0x4060, msg_size @0x4160, urandom @0x41e0, cookie_num @0x41e8. A negative index (e.g. -1 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) computes msg + (-1)*8 = 0x4060 - 8 = 0x4058, which is in the gap between stderr@0x4040 and msg@0x4060. More negative indices reach stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* pointers and other BSS data. This confirms the OOB access reaches meaningful memory.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm at runtime that a large unsigned index (e.g. 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) actually passes the jl check and reaches the array access\",\"Verify what memory contents exist at msg+negative_offset*8 and msg_size+negative_offset*4 to determine what pointer/size values are obtained\",\"Determine whether the OOB-obtained pointer in read_cookie maps to readable memory (for INFO_LEAK) or causes SIGSEGV (for CRASH)\",\"Determine whether the OOB-obtained pointer in edit_cookie maps to writable memory (for OOB_WRITE) or causes SIGSEGV\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-002\",\"title\":\"INTEGER_UNDERFLOW_IN_EDIT_COOKIE_SIZE_CALCULATION\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Integer Underflow\",\"description\":\"create_cookie allows msg_len=0 because the check 'cmp rax,0x100; jle' (at 0x13f6-0x13fc) uses signed jle, and 0 <= 0x100 passes. When msg_len=0, calloc(0,1) allocates a minimum-size heap chunk, and msg_size[cookie_num] is set to 0 (stored as int at 0x14a9). When edit_cookie is later called on this cookie, it computes the read length as msg_size[index]-1. The instruction sequence 'mov eax, [msg_size+index*4]; sub eax,0x1; movsxd rdx,eax' (at 0x16b6-0x16bc) performs 0-1 on a 32-bit int, producing -1 (0xFFFFFFFF), then sign-extends to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF as the 64-bit size argument to read(). This causes read(0, msg[index], 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF), attempting to read an enormous number of bytes into a small heap buffer.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"create_cookie\",\"edit_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg_size[32] @0x4160 (int array, stores allocation size)\",\"msg[32] @0x4060 (char* array, heap pointers)\",\"heap buffer allocated by calloc(0,1)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"msg_len=0 in create_cookie, then edit the created cookie in edit_cookie\",\"entry_function\":\"create_cookie (sets up msg_size=0) → edit_cookie (triggers underflow)\",\"data_flow\":[\"create_cookie: scanf('%lld', &msg_len) at 0x13ed, msg_len=0\",\"create_cookie: cmp rax,0x100; jle 0x140a at 0x13f6-0x13fc — 0 <= 256, passes check\",\"create_cookie: calloc(0,1) at 0x1416 — allocates minimum-size chunk (typically 0x20 on glibc)\",\"create_cookie: msg_size[cookie_num] = 0 (mov DWORD PTR [rdx+rax*1], ecx where ecx=edx=lower 32 bits of msg_len=0) at 0x14a9\",\"create_cookie: cookie_num incremented at 0x14bf-0x14c3\",\"edit_cookie: msg_size[index] loaded into eax at 0x16b6 (eax = 0)\",\"edit_cookie: sub eax,0x1 at 0x16b9 (eax = -1 = 0xFFFFFFFF)\",\"edit_cookie: movsxd rdx,eax at 0x16bc (rdx = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF)\",\"edit_cookie: read(0, msg[index], 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) at 0x16de — massive heap buffer overflow\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"The read length in edit_cookie should be validated to be non-negative and not exceed the actual allocation size. Additionally, create_cookie should reject msg_len=0 or ensure msg_size is at least 1.\",\"actual\":\"create_cookie checks msg_len <= 256 with signed jle, allowing 0 and negative values. edit_cookie computes msg_size[index]-1 without checking if msg_size[index] is 0, producing an underflow to -1 which is sign-extended to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing check for msg_size[index] == 0 (or msg_size[index] < 1) before computing msg_size[index]-1. Missing lower bound check on msg_len in create_cookie (should reject 0 or negative).\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"Heap buffer overflow via integer underflow in size calculation\",\"address\":\"0x16b6-0x16de (edit_cookie size computation and read call)\",\"expression\":\"read(0, msg[index], (int)(msg_size[index]) - 1) where msg_size[index]=0, causing (0-1) = -1, sign-extended to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF\",\"target\":\"heap buffer allocated by calloc(0,1) — minimum-size chunk (typically 0x20 bytes on glibc 2.39)\",\"size\":\"0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (effectively unlimited — read will write until it hits unmapped memory or reaches process memory limit)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The read() call with size 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF writes attacker-controlled stdin data into a minimum-size heap buffer (calloc(0,1) typically yields a 0x20-byte usable chunk). This massively overflows the heap buffer, corrupting adjacent heap metadata and neighboring heap objects.\"},{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The heap overflow corrupts heap chunk metadata (size, prev_size, fd/bk pointers) and adjacent heap allocations. On glibc 2.39, this can corrupt malloc's internal structures.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The massive write will eventually reach unmapped memory and cause SIGSEGV, or corrupt heap metadata leading to a crash on the next malloc/free operation.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x13f6-0x13fc\",\"description\":\"cmp rax,0x100; jle 0x140a — msg_len checked with signed jle against 256. Value 0 passes this check (0 <= 256).\"},{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1416\",\"description\":\"call calloc@plt with rdi=msg_len=0, rsi=1 — calloc(0,1) allocates a minimum-size chunk\"},{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x14a9\",\"description\":\"mov DWORD PTR [rdx+rax*1], ecx — stores msg_len (0) as int into msg_size[cookie_num]. ecx = lower 32 bits of msg_len = 0.\"},{\"id\":\"E013\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16b6-0x16b9\",\"description\":\"mov eax,DWORD PTR [rdx+rax*1] (loads msg_size[index]=0 into eax); sub eax,0x1 (eax = 0-1 = 0xFFFFFFFF = -1 as signed int)\"},{\"id\":\"E014\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16bc\",\"description\":\"movsxd rdx,eax — sign-extends 32-bit -1 to 64-bit 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF. This becomes the size argument to read().\"},{\"id\":\"E015\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"call read@plt with edi=0 (stdin), rsi=msg[index] (small heap buffer), rdx=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (size). This attempts to read ~2^64 bytes into a minimum-size heap buffer.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm at runtime that calloc(0,1) returns a valid pointer and msg_size is stored as 0\",\"Verify that edit_cookie on a msg_size=0 cookie produces read() with size 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF\",\"Determine actual heap layout to assess what adjacent objects/metadata get corrupted\",\"Note: read() may return early if stdin has limited data, but the buffer overflow still occurs for whatever bytes are available\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-003\",\"title\":\"HEAP_USE_AFTER_FREE_IN_EAT_COOKIE_SWAP_REMOVE\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Use-After-Free\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie frees msg[random_index] at 0x158e, then copies msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[random_index] at 0x15c6. When random_index equals cookie_num-1 (i.e., the last cookie is selected for eating), the sequence is: free(msg[cookie_num-1]); msg[cookie_num-1] = msg[cookie_num-1] (writes the freed pointer back to the same slot). The slot now holds a dangling pointer to freed heap memory. cookie_num is decremented, but the old msg[cookie_num-1] slot (now at index cookie_num, which is 'beyond' the active range) is NOT zeroed. The dangling pointer at msg[random_index] (= msg[old_cookie_num-1]) remains accessible if the same index can be reached again. However, after cookie_num decrement, the dangling pointer is at index cookie_num (the old cookie_num-1), which is now outside the valid range [0, cookie_num-1]. Accessing it requires either the signedness bug (BUG-001) to bypass the index check, or creating new cookies to fill the slot.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"eat_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg[32] @0x4060 (char* array)\",\"msg_size[32] @0x4160 (int array)\",\"cookie_num @0x41e8 (long)\",\"heap chunk freed by free(msg[random_index])\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"random_index from /dev/urandom (NOT attacker-controlled)\",\"entry_function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"fread 1 byte from /dev/urandom into rbp-0xc at 0x152b\",\"random_index = byte % cookie_num (idiv at 0x153e) at 0x1530-0x1541\",\"printf('%s', msg[random_index]) at 0x156e — prints cookie content (valid access, before free)\",\"free(msg[random_index]) at 0x158e — frees the heap buffer\",\"msg[random_index] = msg[cookie_num-1] at 0x15b0-0x15c6 — copies last pointer into freed slot\",\"msg_size[random_index] = msg_size[cookie_num-1] at 0x15e7-0x15fc — copies last size into freed slot\",\"cookie_num-- at 0x1606-0x160a\",\"When random_index == cookie_num-1: the freed pointer is written back to its own slot, creating a dangling pointer at msg[random_index]\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"After freeing a cookie, the freed pointer should be set to NULL in the msg[] array, and the old msg[cookie_num-1] slot should be zeroed after the copy to prevent dangling pointers.\",\"actual\":\"After free, the freed pointer is overwritten with msg[cookie_num-1] (swap-remove). When random_index == cookie_num-1, the freed pointer is written back to the same slot. The old msg[cookie_num-1] slot is not zeroed after the move. No NULL-ification of freed pointers occurs.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing NULL-ification of the freed pointer slot after swap-remove. Missing zeroing of the old msg[cookie_num-1] slot after the copy. The swap-remove logic does not handle the self-copy case (random_index == cookie_num-1) correctly.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"Dangling pointer left in msg[] array after free+swap-remove\",\"address\":\"0x158e (free) and 0x15c6 (msg[random_index] = msg[cookie_num-1])\",\"expression\":\"free(msg[random_index]); msg[random_index] = msg[cookie_num-1]; // when random_index == cookie_num-1, this writes freed ptr back\",\"target\":\"msg[random_index] @0x4060+random_index*8 — holds dangling pointer after free\",\"size\":\"8 bytes (pointer)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the dangling pointer at msg[old_cookie_num-1] is accessed via read_cookie (using the signedness bug BUG-001 to bypass the index check, or by creating new cookies that fill the slot), the freed heap memory is read and printed via printf('%s', ptr). This reads from freed/reallocated heap memory.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the dangling pointer is accessed via edit_cookie (using BUG-001 or by creating new cookies), read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1) writes attacker data into the freed heap buffer. If the heap allocator has reused this memory for another allocation, this corrupts the new allocation's data.\"},{\"type\":\"DATA_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the freed heap chunk is reused by a subsequent allocation (calloc/strdup), writing to the dangling pointer corrupts the new object's data, potentially corrupting heap metadata or other heap objects.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the freed memory has been returned to the OS or reallocated, accessing the dangling pointer may cause SIGSEGV or corrupt heap state leading to a crash on subsequent malloc/free operations.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E016\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x158e\",\"description\":\"call free@plt with rdi=msg[random_index] — frees the heap buffer pointed by msg[random_index]\"},{\"id\":\"E017\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x15b0-0x15c6\",\"description\":\"mov rax,QWORD PTR [rcx+rax*1] (loads msg[cookie_num-1]) then mov QWORD PTR [rcx+rdx*1],rax (stores into msg[random_index]). When random_index == cookie_num-1, this reads the freed pointer and writes it back to the same slot.\"},{\"id\":\"E018\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x15ff-0x160a\",\"description\":\"cookie_num is decremented (sub rax,0x1; mov [cookie_num],rax). The old msg[cookie_num-1] slot (now at index cookie_num) is not zeroed — no instruction clears it.\"},{\"id\":\"E019\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x15c6\",\"description\":\"When random_index == cookie_num-1, the freed pointer is written back to msg[random_index]. After cookie_num--, this slot is at index cookie_num (outside active range [0, cookie_num-1]). The dangling pointer persists in the msg[] array and can be accessed via the signedness bug (BUG-001) in edit_cookie/read_cookie, which allows indices >= 0x8000000000000000 to bypass the index check and reach slots at negative offsets. Alternatively, creating new cookies (create_cookie) increments cookie_num and may make the slot accessible again at a valid index.\"}],\"confidence\":\"MEDIUM\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm at runtime that when random_index == cookie_num-1, the freed pointer remains in msg[] after eat_cookie returns\",\"Determine whether the dangling pointer can be practically accessed: via BUG-001 signedness bypass, or via create_cookie incrementing cookie_num to cover the old slot\",\"Verify whether the freed heap chunk gets reused by subsequent allocations (calloc/strdup) before the dangling pointer is accessed\",\"Note: random_index is from /dev/urandom and NOT attacker-controlled, so triggering the random_index == cookie_num-1 condition is probabilistic. However, with cookie_num=1 (after eating 4 cookies), random_index is always 0 == cookie_num-1, guaranteeing the UAF condition.\"]}],\"non_vulnerabilities\":[{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"description\":\"msg_len input validation (upper bound)\",\"reason\":\"msg_len is checked with cmp rax,0x100; jle against 256. Values 1-256 are valid and correctly handled. The upper bound check is sound for positive values. (Note: msg_len=0 is a separate issue tracked as BUG-002.)\"},{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"description\":\"cookie_num overflow check\",\"reason\":\"cookie_num is checked with cmp rax,0x1f; jle against 31. This correctly limits the number of cookies to 32 (indices 0-31), preventing overflow of the msg[32] and msg_size[32] arrays.\"},{\"function\":\"main\",\"description\":\"choice input dispatch\",\"reason\":\"main reads choice as %lld and uses cmp/je chain for values 1-5 with default case looping back. No memory operations are performed on the choice value beyond the switch dispatch. No vulnerability.\"},{\"function\":\"menu\",\"description\":\"menu display\",\"reason\":\"menu only calls puts() with static string literals. No user input is processed. No vulnerability.\"},{\"function\":\"init\",\"description\":\"initialization\",\"reason\":\"init calls setvbuf, alarm, fopen, and strdup with hardcoded strings. The urandom NULL check is present. All memory writes target fixed BSS locations with correct sizes. No vulnerability.\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether seccomp or other sandbox is active\",\"reason\":\"No seccomp filter detected in ELF by S1. Cannot confirm runtime sandbox without dynamic analysis.\",\"required_next_step\":\"S3 should check for seccomp at runtime (e.g., via seccomp-tools or strace)\"},{\"description\":\"Exact heap layout and chunk addresses at runtime\",\"reason\":\"ASLR + heap randomization make static determination of heap layout impossible. The impact of BUG-002 (what gets corrupted) and BUG-003 (what occupies the freed chunk) depend on runtime heap state.\",\"required_next_step\":\"S3 should perform dynamic heap analysis to determine heap layout and chunk adjacency\"},{\"description\":\"Practical exploitability of BUG-001 OOB access\",\"reason\":\"The OOB access via negative index reaches memory before msg[] in BSS (stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* pointers at 0x4020-0x4040, gap at 0x4048-0x405f). The values at these locations determine whether the OOB-obtained pointer is valid for read/write. Need runtime verification of BSS contents.\",\"required_next_step\":\"S3 should verify BSS memory contents at runtime and test specific negative index values to determine what pointers/sizes are obtained\"},{\"description\":\"Whether BUG-003 UAF can be reliably triggered\",\"reason\":\"random_index is from /dev/urandom. The condition random_index == cookie_num-1 is probabilistic in general, but deterministic when cookie_num=1 (after eating 4 of 5 initial cookies). S3 should verify this scenario.\",\"required_next_step\":\"S3 should test the scenario where cookie_num is reduced to 1, making random_index always 0 == cookie_num-1, guaranteeing the UAF\"}]},\"primitive_reports\":[{\"schema\":\"PrimitiveReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"stage\":\"S3\",\"target_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"analyzed_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"total_primitives\":4,\"verified\":4,\"theoretical\":0,\"candidate\":0,\"unknown\":0},\"primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-001\",\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch in index validation: edit_cookie and read_cookie read index via scanf %llu (unsigned 64-bit) but validate with signed jl comparison. An unsigned value with high bit set (e.g. 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC = -4 signed) passes the < cookie_num check, enabling OOB access to BSS data before the msg[] array.\",\"description\":\"read_cookie with negative index -4 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC) accesses msg[-4] at address 0x4040, which holds the stderr FILE* pointer (libc address). This pointer is dereferenced by printf('%s', msg[index]), leaking bytes from the FILE structure in libc memory.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"Signedness mismatch: %llu input + signed jl comparison\",\"Negative index -4 bypasses index < cookie_num check\",\"msg[-4] at 0x4040 = stderr FILE* pointer (libc address)\",\"printf('%s', stderr_ptr) dereferences and prints FILE structure bytes\",\"INFO_LEAK\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Index value passed to read_cookie via scanf %llu\",\"how\":\"Attacker sends unsigned representation of -4 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC) as the cookie index. The signed jl comparison treats this as -4 < 5 (cookie_num), passing the check. The index is used to compute msg + (-4)*8 = 0x4060 - 32 = 0x4040, which is the stderr FILE* global.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"Index with high bit set (>= 0x8000000000000000) must be accepted by scanf %llu and pass the signed jl comparison against cookie_num\",\"necessity\":\"Without bypassing the index check, no OOB access occurs. The signed jl treats high-bit-set values as negative, which is always < positive cookie_num.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"The OOB slot msg[-4] at 0x4040 must contain a valid (non-NULL) pointer to readable memory\",\"necessity\":\"printf('%s', ptr) dereferences the pointer. If NULL or unmapped, SIGSEGV occurs instead of info leak. stderr FILE* is initialized by init() via setvbuf, so it points to a valid libc FILE structure.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"cookie_num must be > 0 (at least one cookie exists) for the index check to have a positive comparison value\",\"necessity\":\"If cookie_num=0, the jl comparison would be index < 0, and even negative indices would fail. init() sets cookie_num=5, satisfying this.\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-4] = *(msg_base + (-4)*8) = *(0x4060 - 32) = *(0x4040)\",\"alias\":\"stderr FILE* global variable at BSS 0x4040\",\"implication\":\"The stderr FILE* pointer (pointing to libc _IO_2_1_stderr_ structure) is dereferenced and its contents are printed as a string, leaking libc internal FILE structure bytes.\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Run read_cookie with index -4 (unsigned 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC). Capture printf output which contains bytes from the stderr FILE structure in libc.\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled, ASLR on\",\"run_command\":\"cd /work/test && python3 poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"Leaked bytes from stderr FILE structure (non-zero, non-attacker-provided data)\",\"Hex dump of leaked bytes showing libc FILE structure contents\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE/ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes the binary base address, but the OOB access is relative to the msg[] array in BSS, so PIE does not prevent the OOB access itself. The leaked data comes from libc FILE structures, which contain libc addresses that can be used to defeat ASLR.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only, but this primitive is a read (info leak), not a GOT write. No impact on this primitive.\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"NX prevents code execution on stack/heap, but this is an info leak primitive. No direct impact.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Canary protects return addresses, but this primitive does not involve stack overflow. No impact.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17aa-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"cmp rdx,rax; jl 0x17bb — signed comparison on unsigned %llu input allows negative indices to pass the < cookie_num check\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bf-0x17e1\",\"description\":\"lea rdx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+0x2892] (msg@0x4060); mov rax,[rdx+rax] — computes msg+index*8, dereferences as pointer for printf %s\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"runtime\",\"description\":\"read_cookie(-4) leaked 6 bytes from stderr FILE structure: hex 8720adfb0a0a. These are non-attacker-provided bytes from libc FILE structure, confirming INFO_LEAK.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"8720adfb0a0a (6 bytes from stderr FILE structure)\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"PRIM-001 VERIFIED: read_cookie(-4) leaked 6 bytes from stderr FILE structure (hex: 8720adfb0a0a). The leaked bytes are from libc's _IO_2_1_stderr_ FILE structure, not attacker-provided data. This confirms INFO_LEAK via OOB pointer dereference.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"The leak is from the stderr FILE structure in libc. The 6 bytes leaked (before hitting a NUL byte) contain libc internal pointers. Combined with PRIM-003 (PIE base leak), this provides both PIE and libc address information for defeating ASLR.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-002\",\"type\":\"RELATIVE_WRITE\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch in edit_cookie index validation. Negative index -11 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF5) passes the signed jl check. msg[-11] at address 0x4008 is __dso_handle, a self-referencing global pointer (*(&__dso_handle) == &__dso_handle). edit_cookie writes to *msg[-11] = *(__dso_handle) = __dso_handle's own address, overwriting the __dso_handle value.\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie with negative index -11 accesses msg[-11] at 0x4008 (__dso_handle). The read() call writes attacker data to the address stored in msg[-11], which is __dso_handle's own address (self-referencing). This overwrites the __dso_handle global variable with attacker-controlled data.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"Signedness mismatch: %llu input + signed jl comparison\",\"Negative index -11 bypasses index < cookie_num check\",\"msg[-11] at 0x4008 = __dso_handle (self-referencing: value == &__dso_handle)\",\"read(0, msg[-11], msg_size[-11]-1) writes to *(__dso_handle) = __dso_handle's own address\",\"__dso_handle value overwritten with attacker data\",\"RELATIVE_WRITE to fixed global address\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Written value (data sent to stdin for read())\",\"how\":\"Attacker sends data via stdin which is written to the address stored in msg[-11] (= __dso_handle address). The write target is the fixed global __dso_handle at PIE+0x4008, not an attacker-chosen address.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-04\",\"description\":\"Index -11 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF5) must pass the signed jl comparison against cookie_num\",\"necessity\":\"Required for OOB access. -11 < 5 (cookie_num) in signed comparison, so it passes.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-05\",\"description\":\"msg[-11] at 0x4008 must contain a valid writable pointer (self-referencing __dso_handle)\",\"necessity\":\"read() writes to *msg[-11]. __dso_handle is self-referencing (value == &__dso_handle), so the write target is __dso_handle's own address in .data section (writable).\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-06\",\"description\":\"msg_size[-11] must be a positive value to produce a non-negative read size (msg_size[-11]-1 >= 0)\",\"necessity\":\"msg_size[-11] at 0x4160-44 = 0x4134 aliases msg[26]'s high 4 bytes. Initially this is 0 (uninitialized). Creating 22 cookies (indices 5-26) fills msg[26] with heap pointers whose high 4 bytes are ~0x5555 (PIE high bytes), making msg_size[-11] = ~21845, a small positive number. read size = 21844.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-07\",\"description\":\"The written value must be a readable address for the newline check to pass without crash\",\"necessity\":\"After read(), edit_cookie checks *(msg[-11] + read_return_value) for newline. If the written value is not a readable address, this dereference causes SIGSEGV. Writing a readable BSS address (e.g., dso_addr+8) avoids the crash.\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-11] = *(msg_base + (-11)*8) = *(0x4060 - 88) = *(0x4008)\",\"alias\":\"__dso_handle global variable at .data 0x4008\",\"implication\":\"__dso_handle is self-referencing: its value equals its own address (&__dso_handle). Writing to *msg[-11] writes to __dso_handle's own address, overwriting its value. This is the foundation for the two-step ARB_WRITE (PRIM-004).\"},{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-11] = *(msg_size_base + (-11)*4) = *(0x4160 - 44) = *(0x4134)\",\"alias\":\"High 4 bytes of msg[26] (at 0x4060 + 26*8 = 0x4130, high 4 bytes at 0x4134)\",\"implication\":\"msg_size[-11] aliases the high 4 bytes of msg[26]. When msg[26] contains a heap pointer (e.g., 0x0000555555xxxxxx), the high 4 bytes are ~0x5555 (21845), a small positive number. This makes msg_size[-11]-1 = 21844, a valid read size. Requires creating 22 cookies (indices 5-26) to fill msg[26].\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"1. Create 22 cookies to fill msg[26], making msg_size[-11] = ~21845. 2. Call edit_cookie(-11) with a readable address as data. 3. Verify __dso_handle value changed via /proc/pid/mem.\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled, ASLR on\",\"run_command\":\"cd /work/test && python3 poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"__dso_handle value changed from self-referencing address to attacker-written value\",\"msg_size[-11] = small positive number (~21845) after creating 22 cookies\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE/ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes the base address, but the OOB access is relative to BSS. The write target (__dso_handle) is at a fixed PIE offset (0x4008). The written value must be a valid readable address, which requires knowing PIE base (obtainable via PRIM-003).\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only. __dso_handle is in .data (writable), not GOT, so RELRO does not prevent this write.\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"NX is irrelevant for a data write primitive.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Canary is irrelevant for a heap/BSS write primitive.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684\",\"description\":\"cmp rdx,rax; jl — signed comparison allows negative index -11 to pass\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16c3-0x16d2\",\"description\":\"lea rcx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+0x298e] (msg@0x4060); mov rax,[rcx+rax] — computes msg+(-11)*8 = 0x4008 = __dso_handle, dereferences as write target\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"runtime\",\"description\":\"After creating 22 cookies, msg_size[-11] = 23044 (0x5a04). edit_cookie(-11) wrote 0x5a03dfaee010 (dso_addr+8) to __dso_handle. Original value was 0x5a03dfaee008 (self-ref). Write to fixed global __dso_handle confirmed via /proc/pid/mem readback.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"0x5a03dfaee010 (dso_addr+8, a readable BSS address)\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"PRIM-002 VERIFIED: edit_cookie(-11) wrote to __dso_handle (fixed global at PIE+0x4008). Original self-ref value 0x5a03dfaee008 changed to 0x5a03dfaee010. msg_size[-11] = 23044 (small positive, from msg[26] high bytes). Write target is fixed global (not attacker-chosen), so this is RELATIVE_WRITE, not ARB_WRITE. The ARB_WRITE upgrade is in PRIM-004.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"This is RELATIVE_WRITE (fixed target = __dso_handle), not ARB_WRITE. The write target is the fixed global __dso_handle at PIE+0x4008. The written value is attacker-controlled. The self-referencing property of __dso_handle is the key enabler for the two-step ARB_WRITE in PRIM-004.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-003\",\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch in read_cookie index validation. Negative index -11 accesses msg[-11] at 0x4008 (__dso_handle). __dso_handle is self-referencing (value == &__dso_handle == PIE_base + 0x4008). printf('%s', msg[-11]) prints the bytes at the __dso_handle address, which are the __dso_handle value itself (little-endian address bytes), leaking the PIE base address.\",\"description\":\"read_cookie with negative index -11 dereferences msg[-11] = __dso_handle value = PIE_base + 0x4008. printf('%s', PIE_base + 0x4008) prints the bytes at that address, which are the little-endian representation of the address itself (self-referencing). This leaks the PIE base address.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"Signedness mismatch: %llu input + signed jl comparison\",\"Negative index -11 bypasses index < cookie_num check\",\"msg[-11] at 0x4008 = __dso_handle (self-referencing: value == &__dso_handle == PIE+0x4008)\",\"printf('%s', PIE+0x4008) prints bytes at that address = the address itself in LE\",\"PIE base address leaked (PIE_base = leaked_value - 0x4008)\",\"INFO_LEAK (PIE base)\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Index value passed to read_cookie via scanf %llu\",\"how\":\"Attacker sends unsigned representation of -11 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF5) as the cookie index. The signed jl comparison treats this as -11 < 5 (cookie_num), passing the check. The index computes msg + (-11)*8 = 0x4060 - 88 = 0x4008 = __dso_handle.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-08\",\"description\":\"Index -11 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF5) must pass the signed jl comparison against cookie_num\",\"necessity\":\"Required for OOB access. -11 < 5 in signed comparison, passes.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-09\",\"description\":\"msg[-11] at 0x4008 must contain a valid readable pointer\",\"necessity\":\"printf('%s', ptr) dereferences the pointer. __dso_handle is self-referencing (value == &__dso_handle), pointing to .data section (readable). Always satisfied.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-10\",\"description\":\"__dso_handle must be self-referencing (value == &__dso_handle)\",\"necessity\":\"The leaked bytes are the __dso_handle value. If self-referencing, the leaked bytes are the PIE base + 0x4008 address, directly revealing PIE base. __dso_handle is initialized by the linker to be self-referencing.\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-11] = *(0x4060 + (-11)*8) = *(0x4008) = __dso_handle\",\"alias\":\"__dso_handle self-referencing pointer at .data 0x4008\",\"implication\":\"__dso_handle's value equals its own address (PIE_base + 0x4008). When dereferenced by printf('%s'), the bytes printed are the little-endian representation of PIE_base + 0x4008, directly leaking the PIE base address.\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Run read_cookie with index -11 (unsigned 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF5). Capture printf output which contains the __dso_handle address in little-endian. Compute PIE_base = leaked_value - 0x4008.\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled, ASLR on\",\"run_command\":\"cd /work/test && python3 poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"Leaked bytes matching PIE_base + 0x4008 in little-endian\",\"PIE base correctly recovered from leaked bytes\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE/ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes the base address, but this primitive directly leaks the PIE base, defeating PIE/ASLR for the binary. The leaked __dso_handle address reveals PIE_base = leaked_value - 0x4008.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact on a read/info-leak primitive.\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact on an info leak primitive.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"No impact on an info leak primitive.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17aa-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"cmp rdx,rax; jl — signed comparison allows negative index -11 to pass\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bf-0x17e1\",\"description\":\"lea rdx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+0x2892] (msg@0x4060); mov rax,[rdx+rax] — computes msg+(-11)*8 = 0x4008 = __dso_handle, dereferences as pointer for printf %s\"},{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"runtime\",\"description\":\"read_cookie(-11) leaked bytes 08a009c598570a0a. First 6 bytes (08a009c59857) match __dso_handle address 0x5798c509a008 (PIE_base 0x5798c5096000 + 0x4008) in little-endian. PIE base successfully recovered.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"08a009c598570a0a (6 bytes = __dso_handle address in LE = PIE_base + 0x4008)\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"PRIM-003 VERIFIED: read_cookie(-11) leaked 6 bytes (08a009c598570a0a) which are the little-endian representation of __dso_handle address (0x5798c509a008 = PIE_base 0x5798c5096000 + 0x4008). The self-referencing property of __dso_handle causes printf to print the address itself. PIE base = leaked_value - 0x4008 = 0x5798c5096000, confirmed correct via /proc/pid/maps.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"This primitive directly defeats PIE by leaking the binary's base address. The self-referencing __dso_handle at PIE+0x4008 is the key enabler: its value equals its own address, so dereferencing it as a string pointer prints the address bytes. The leak stops at the first NUL byte (high bytes of the 64-bit address are 0x00), yielding ~6 bytes of the address.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-004\",\"type\":\"ARB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"INDIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch in edit_cookie index validation. Negative index -11 accesses msg[-11] at 0x4008 (__dso_handle, self-referencing). Using a two-step write technique: Step 1 overwrites __dso_handle with an attacker-chosen address Y; Step 2 uses the same edit_cookie(-11) to write data to *(Y), since msg[-11] now contains Y. This upgrades RELATIVE_WRITE (PRIM-002) to ARB_WRITE.\",\"description\":\"Two-step ARB_WRITE via __dso_handle self-reference: Step 1: edit_cookie(-11) writes Y (attacker-chosen target address) to __dso_handle. Since __dso_handle is self-referencing, the write target is __dso_handle's own address, so __dso_handle's value becomes Y. Step 2: edit_cookie(-11) again — now msg[-11] = Y, so read() writes attacker data to address Y. The written value Y must be a readable address for the newline check to pass.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"Signedness mismatch: %llu input + signed jl comparison\",\"Negative index -11 bypasses index < cookie_num check\",\"msg[-11] at 0x4008 = __dso_handle (self-referencing)\",\"Step 1: edit_cookie(-11) writes Y to *(__dso_handle) = __dso_handle's own address -> __dso_handle = Y\",\"Step 2: edit_cookie(-11) writes data to *(Y) since msg[-11] now = Y\",\"Write lands at attacker-chosen address Y -> ARB_WRITE\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Both the write target address (Y) and the written data\",\"how\":\"Step 1: Attacker sends Y (target address) as the data for read(). Since __dso_handle is self-referencing, read() writes Y to __dso_handle's own address, changing __dso_handle's value to Y. Step 2: Attacker sends payload data. Now msg[-11] = Y, so read() writes payload to address Y. The target address Y is fully attacker-controlled (must be readable+writable for the newline check to pass).\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-11\",\"description\":\"Index -11 must pass the signed jl comparison (same as PRIM-002)\",\"necessity\":\"Required for OOB access to msg[-11] = __dso_handle.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-12\",\"description\":\"__dso_handle must be self-referencing (value == &__dso_handle)\",\"necessity\":\"The two-step technique requires that the initial write target is __dso_handle's own address. Self-referencing means *(__dso_handle) writes to __dso_handle itself, allowing the attacker to change __dso_handle's value to Y in step 1.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-13\",\"description\":\"msg_size[-11] must be a small positive number (requires creating 22 cookies to fill msg[26])\",\"necessity\":\"msg_size[-11] aliases msg[26]'s high 4 bytes. Creating 22 cookies (indices 5-26) fills msg[26] with heap pointers whose high bytes are ~0x5555, making msg_size[-11] = ~21845. read size = 21844, sufficient for writing 8-byte addresses and payloads.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-14\",\"description\":\"The target address Y must be readable for the newline check to pass without crash\",\"necessity\":\"After read(), edit_cookie checks *(msg[-11] + read_return_value) for newline. In step 1, msg[-11] is still __dso_handle (self-ref), so the check accesses *(dso_addr + N) which is readable (.data section). In step 2, msg[-11] = Y, so the check accesses *(Y + N) — Y must be readable. Choosing Y in BSS/.data (always readable) satisfies this.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-15\",\"description\":\"The target address Y must be writable\",\"necessity\":\"read() writes to *(Y) in step 2. Y must be in a writable memory region (BSS, .data, heap).\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-11] = *(0x4008) = __dso_handle (self-referencing: *dso == &dso)\",\"alias\":\"__dso_handle self-referencing pointer\",\"implication\":\"The self-referencing property is the key enabler for two-step ARB_WRITE. Step 1 writes to *(__dso_handle) = __dso_handle's own address (because value == &itself), changing the value to Y. Step 2 writes to *(Y) because msg[-11] now contains Y. This upgrades RELATIVE_WRITE (fixed target) to ARB_WRITE (attacker-chosen target).\"},{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-11] = *(0x4134) = high 4 bytes of msg[26]\",\"alias\":\"msg[26] high half (PIE high bytes ~0x5555)\",\"implication\":\"Creating 22 cookies fills msg[26] with heap pointers. The high 4 bytes (~0x5555 = 21845) alias to msg_size[-11], producing a small positive read size (21844) that passes the size computation. This is the size dimension of the OOB access.\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Two-step write to two different attacker-chosen addresses (Y1=PIE+0x4010, Y2=PIE+0x4068). For each: Step 1 writes Y to __dso_handle via edit_cookie(-11). Step 2 writes marker data to *(Y) via edit_cookie(-11). Verify via /proc/pid/mem readback.\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled, ASLR on\",\"run_command\":\"cd /work/test && python3 poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"Y1 = PIE+0x4010: marker 0x4141414142424242 written and readback confirmed\",\"Y2 = PIE+0x4068: marker 0x4343434344444444 written and readback confirmed\",\"Two different addresses successfully written = ARB_WRITE\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE/ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes addresses, but PRIM-003 leaks the PIE base, allowing the attacker to compute valid readable+writable target addresses (Y) in BSS/.data. ASLR does not prevent this primitive once PIE base is known.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only. The two-step write targets BSS/.data addresses (writable), not GOT. RELRO does not prevent writing to BSS/.data. However, GOT overwrite is not possible via this primitive.\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"NX prevents executing shellcode, but this is a write primitive. The written data could be used for other purposes (e.g., overwriting function pointers in writable BSS), but NX prevents direct shellcode execution.\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Canary protects stack return addresses. This primitive writes to BSS/.data, not stack. No impact.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684\",\"description\":\"cmp rdx,rax; jl — signed comparison allows negative index -11 to pass\"},{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16c3-0x16de\",\"description\":\"lea rcx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+0x298e] (msg@0x4060); mov rax,[rcx+rax] — computes msg+(-11)*8 = 0x4008 = __dso_handle; read(0, msg[-11], msg_size[-11]-1) writes to *(__dso_handle)\"},{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"runtime\",\"description\":\"Two-step ARB_WRITE verified with two distinct addresses: Y1=PIE+0x4010 (0x611057cd6010): wrote 0x4141414142424242, readback confirmed 4141414142424242. Y2=PIE+0x4068 (0x61762ea87068): wrote 0x4343434344444444, readback confirmed 4343434344444444. Two different attacker-chosen addresses successfully written.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"Y1: 0x4141414142424242 at PIE+0x4010; Y2: 0x4343434344444444 at PIE+0x4068\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"PRIM-004 VERIFIED: Two-step ARB_WRITE via __dso_handle self-reference. Step 1: edit_cookie(-11) writes target address Y to __dso_handle (self-ref: write target = __dso_handle's own address). Step 2: edit_cookie(-11) writes payload to *(Y) since msg[-11] now = Y. Verified with two distinct attacker-chosen addresses: Y1=PIE+0x4010 wrote 0x4141414142424242 (readback: 4141414142424242), Y2=PIE+0x4068 wrote 0x4343434344444444 (readback: 4343434344444444). Both writes confirmed via /proc/pid/mem readback. This meets ARB_WRITE acceptance criteria: two different attacker-chosen addresses successfully written.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"This is the highest-level primitive derived from BUG-001. The two-step technique leverages __dso_handle's self-referencing property: Step 1 changes __dso_handle's value to the attacker's chosen target Y (the write goes to __dso_handle's own address because *dso == &dso). Step 2 writes payload data to *(Y) because msg[-11] now contains Y. The target address Y is fully attacker-controlled (must be readable for the newline check, writable for the write to succeed). This upgrades RELATIVE_WRITE (PRIM-002, fixed target) to ARB_WRITE (attacker-chosen target). Each two-step write requires a fresh process because step 1 modifies __dso_handle, breaking the self-referencing property for subsequent writes to different targets.\"}],\"non_primitives\":[],\"unresolved\":[]}],\"target_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"bug_reports\":[],\"impact_reports\":[],\"strategy_reports\":[],\"final_exploit_path\":\"\",\"final_report\":{}}",
